{"timestamp":"2026-10-03T02:55:30.481Z","ordinal":268,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a0ffb0-2ab1-7380-8920-faef4e2d1ef9","call_id":"call_fpipAqfSSySz5jHGJCDspnEr","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 1.7 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"hou.hipFile (https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/hom/hou/hipFile.html)\nciteturn4view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/hom/hou/hipFile.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 2964\nL0: cite0†Houdini 22.0 cite1†Python scripting cite2†hou L1: \nL2: # hou.hipFile module\nL3: \nL4: Functions for working with the current scene (`.hip`) file.\nL5: \nL6: Note\nL7: \nL8: Houdini inherits the “current directory” (sometimes called the “current working directory”) from the environment where you run it. Some functions return or accept paths relative to the current directory. You can check Houdini’s current directory by calling `os.getcwd()`, and change it by calling `os.chdir`.\nL9: ## Functions cite3†¶ L10: \nL11: `basename() ` → `str `\nL12: \nL13: Return the filename portion of the current scene’s path (including the `.hip` extension).\nL14: \nL15: Return the filename portion of the current scene’s path (including the .hip extension).\nL16: \nL17: `name() ` → `str `\nL18: \nL19: Return the name of the current hip file.\nL20: This function may or may not return an absolute path. If the hip file has been loaded with a relative path, then this will return a path relative to `os.getcwd()`. Otherwise, if the hip file has been loaded with an absolute path, then this will also return the absolute path. Calling cite4†hou.hipFile.setName will set the name returned by this function.\nL21: \nL22: If you want an absolute path to the current scene file, use cite5†hou.hipFile.path instead.\nL23: \nL24: Return the name of the current hip file.\nL25: \nL26: `path() ` → `str `\nL27: Return the absolute file path of the current scene file. Remember that a file may not exist at this path if the current scene hasn’t been saved yet.\nL28: \nL29: Return the absolute file path of the current scene file.\nL30: \nL31: `hasUnsavedChanges() ` → `bool `\nL32: \nL33: Returns whether the current Houdini session has been modified since it was last saved. (This is sometimes referred to as the current file being “dirty”.)\nL34: This function only works in a graphical session. In a non-graphical session (`hython`) it will always return True.\nL35: \nL36: Returns whether the current Houdini session has been modified since it was last saved.\nL37: \nL38: `isNewFile() ` → `bool `\nL39: \nL40: Returns whether the current Houdini file is a new file or is a previously loaded file.\nL41: \nL42: This function only works accurately in a graphical session. In a non-graphical session (`hython`) it will always return True if the filename matches the default filename.\nL43: Returns whether the current Houdini file is a new file or is a previously loaded file.\nL44: \nL45: `load(file_name, suppress_save_prompt=False, ignore_load_warnings=False) `\nL46: \nL47: Loads a new scene (`.hip`) file.\nL48: \nL49: Raises cite6†hou.OperationFailed if Houdini cannot read the file.\nL50: \nL51: Raises cite7†hou.LoadWarning if loading the new file triggers warnings (such as missing assets). You can prevent these exceptions by passing `ignore_load_warnings=True`.\nL52: \nL53: `file_name`\nL54: \nL55: The path to the scene file to load.\nL56: \nL57: `suppress_save_prompt`\nL58: Normally, in a graphical session, this function acts the same as if the user had chosen File ▸ Open, and prompts to save the current file if it has unsaved changes. You can force Houdini to load the new file without prompting by passing `suppress_save_prompt=True`.\nL59: \nL60: In a non-graphical session (`hython`), the function always acts like `suppress_save_prompt` is on.\nL61: \nL62: `ignore_load_warnings`\nL63: Prevents Houdini from raising cite7†hou.LoadWarning exceptions if loading the new file triggers warnings (such as missing assets).\nL64: \nL65: Raises cite6†hou.OperationFailed if you call this while Houdini is cooking a node, shutting down (see cite8†hou.hipFile.isShuttingDown ) or if Houdini is saving the scene file. If this method is called when loading a hip file (from 456.py), the operation will succeed, but after merging the hip file the 456.py script will not be run again.\nL66: \nL67: Loads a new scene (.hip) file.\nL68: `save(file_name=None, save_to_recent_files=True) `\nL69: \nL70: Saves the current scene to a `.hip` file.\nL71: \nL72: `file_name`\nL73: \nL74: If this is a string, saves the current scene to a file at this path, like File ▸ Save As. The path can be absolute or relative to the current directory.\nL75: \nL76: If this is `None`, saves the scene to its current path (cite5†hou.hipFile.path ), like File ▸ Save.\nL77: \nL78: If intermediate directories in the path do not exist, Houdini will create them.\nL79: \nL80: `save_to_recent_files`\nL81: In a graphical session, whether to add this file to the list of recent files in the File ▸ Open Recent Files menu. The default is True.\nL82: \nL83: In a non-graphical session (`hython`), the function always acts like `save_to_recent_files` is off.\nL84: \nL85: Raises cite6†hou.OperationFailed if Houdini can’t create intermediate directories or write to the specified file (for example, because of filesystem permissions).\nL86: Also raises cite6†hou.OperationFailed if you call this while Houdini is shutting down (see cite8†hou.hipFile.isShuttingDown ) or if Houdini is already loading or saving the scene file (see cite9†hou.hipFile.isLoadingHipFile ).\nL87: \nL88: Saves the current scene to a .hip file.\nL89: \nL90: `setName(file_name) `\nL91: \nL92: Sets the in-memory path of the current scene file. This is the path Houdini will save to if the user chooses File ▸ Save or you call cite10†hou.hipFile.save without a file path.\nL93: \nL94: `file_name`\nL95: The path to use. This can be an absolute path or relative to the current directory.\nL96: \nL97: This function does not save the scene, it only changes the path Houdini thinks the scene should be saved at. To actually save the scene to a new path, use cite10†hou.hipFile.save .\nL98: \nL99: Sets the in-memory path of the current scene file.\nL100: \nL101: `saveAndIncrementFileName() `\nL102: \nL103: Saves the scene to its current path (same as File ▸ Save), but increments a number at the end of the filename.\nL104: Raises cite6†hou.OperationFailed if Houdini can’t create intermediate directories or write to the specified file (for example, because of filesystem permissions).\nL105: \nL106: Also raises cite6†hou.OperationFailed if you call this while Houdini is shutting down (see cite8†hou.hipFile.isShuttingDown ) or if Houdini is already loading or saving the scene file (see cite9†hou.hipFile.isLoadingHipFile ).\nL107: \nL108: Saves the scene to its current path (same as File ▸ Save), but increments a number at the end of the filename.\nL109: `saveAndBackup() ` → `str `\nL110: \nL111: Saves the current scene but first creates a backup of the previous saved state to a backup file in `$HOUDINI_BACKUP_DIR` with `_bak` and an increasing number added before the `.hip` extension. If `$HOUDINI_BACKUP_DIR` is not set, then this method creates the backup file in a `backup` directory in the same directory as the scene file.\nL112: \nL113: Returns the absolute path of the backup file.\nL114: Raises cite6†hou.OperationFailed if Houdini can’t create intermediate directories or write to the specified file (for example, because of filesystem permissions).\nL115: \nL116: Also raises cite6†hou.OperationFailed if you call this while Houdini is shutting down (see cite8†hou.hipFile.isShuttingDown ) or if Houdini is already loading or saving the scene file (see cite9†hou.hipFile.isLoadingHipFile ).\nL117: Saves the current scene but first creates a backup of the previous saved state to a backup file in $HOUDINI_BACKUP_DIR with _bak and an increasing number added before the .hip extension.\nL118: \nL119: `saveAsBackup() ` → `str `\nL120: \nL121: Saves the current scene to a new file in `$HOUDINI_BACKUP_DIR` with `_bak` and an increasing number added before the `.hip` extension. If `$HOUDINI_BACKUP_DIR` is not set, this saves the file to a `backup` directory in the same directory as the scene file.\nL122: Returns the absolute path of the backup file.\nL123: \nL124: Raises cite6†hou.OperationFailed if Houdini can’t create intermediate directories or write to the specified file (for example, because of filesystem permissions).\nL125: \nL126: Also raises cite6†hou.OperationFailed if you call this while Houdini is shutting down (see cite8†hou.hipFile.isShuttingDown ) or if Houdini is already loading or saving the scene file (see cite9†hou.hipFile.isLoadingHipFile ).\nL127: Saves the current scene to a new file in $HOUDINI_BACKUP_DIR with _bak and an increasing number added before the .hip extension.\nL128: \nL129: `clear(suppress_save_prompt=False) ` → `bool `\nL130: \nL131: Clears the contents of the current scene file, starting a new unsaved file. Returns `True` if the contents were cleared and `False` otherwise. The contents will not be cleared, if, in a graphic session, the user chooses to cancel when prompted to save the current file.\nL132: \nL133: `suppress_save_prompt`\nL134: Normally, in a graphical session, this function acts the same as if the user had chosen File ▸ New, and prompts to save the current file if it has unsaved changes. You can force Houdini to clear without prompting by passing `suppress_save_prompt=True`.\nL135: \nL136: In a non-graphical session (`hython`), the function always acts like `suppress_save_prompt` is on.\nL137: \nL138: Clears the contents of the current scene file, starting a new unsaved file.\nL139: `merge(file_name, node_pattern=\"*\", overwrite_on_conflict=False, ignore_load_warnings=False) `\nL140: \nL141: Imports the contents of the file at path `file_name` into the current scene. (This does not save the current scene file.)\nL142: \nL143: Raises cite7†hou.LoadWarning if loading the new file triggers warnings (such as missing assets). You can prevent these exceptions by passing `ignore_load_warnings=True`.\nL144: \nL145: `node_pattern`\nL146: \nL147: Only merge in nodes matching this pattern.\nL148: \nL149: `overwrite_on_conflict`\nL150: What to do if merged-in nodes have the same path/name as existing nodes. If this is `True`, merged in nodes replace existing nodes. If this is `False` (the default), merged in nodes are renamed if the conflict with existing nodes.\nL151: \nL152: See cite11†hou.hipFile.collisionNodesIfMerged to check for conflicts before merging.\nL153: \nL154: `ignore_load_warnings`\nL155: \nL156: Prevents Houdini from raising cite7†hou.LoadWarning exceptions if loading the new file triggers warnings (such as missing assets).\nL157: Raises cite6†hou.OperationFailed if the file to merge doesn’t exist or Houdini cannot read the file.\nL158: \nL159: Also raises cite6†hou.OperationFailed if you call this while Houdini is cooking a node, shutting down (see cite8†hou.hipFile.isShuttingDown ) or if Houdini is saving the scene file. If this method is called when loading a hip file (from 456.py), the operation will succeed, but after merging the hip file the 456.py script will not be run again.\nL160: Imports the contents of the file at path file_name into the current scene.\nL161: \nL162: `collisionNodesIfMerged(file_name, node_pattern=\"*\") ` → tuple of cite12†hou.OpNode L163: \nL164: Returns a sequence of cite12†hou.OpNode objects representing the nodes that would have conflicts if you tried to merge the given file with cite13†hou.hipFile.merge .\nL165: \nL166: `node_pattern`\nL167: \nL168: Only check nodes matching this pattern.\nL169: \nL170: Raises cite6†hou.OperationFailed if the file to merge doesn’t exist or Houdini cannot read the file.\nL171: Also raises cite6†hou.OperationFailed if you call this while Houdini is shutting down (see cite8†hou.hipFile.isShuttingDown ) or if Houdini is already loading or saving the scene file (see cite9†hou.hipFile.isLoadingHipFile ).\nL172: \nL173: Returns a sequence of hou.OpNode objects representing the nodes that would have conflicts if you tried to merge the given file with hou.hipFile.merge.\nL174: \nL175: `isLoadingHipFile() ` → `bool `\nL176: \nL177: Returns `True` if Houdini is currently loading a scene file.\nL178: Returns True if Houdini is currently loading a scene file.\nL179: \nL180: `isShuttingDown() ` → `bool `\nL181: \nL182: Returns `True` if Houdini is currently exiting.\nL183: \nL184: Returns True if Houdini is currently exiting.\nL185: \nL186: `groupColorTable() ` → `dict ` of `str ` to cite14†hou.Color L187: Returns a dictionary of color overrides for the viewport group list. The viewport group list automatically assigns colors to groups based on a hash of the group name. The group color table is a dictionary of overrides to these default color assignments. This allows particular important groups to be assigned distinctive colors. The group color table is saved with the hip file. The color table can be modified with cite15†hou.hipFile.setGroupColorTable .\nL188: Returns a dictionary of color overrides for the viewport group list.\nL189: \nL190: `setGroupColorTable(color_table) `\nL191: Sets a dictionary of color overrides for the viewport group list. The viewport group list automatically assigns colors to groups based on a hash of the group name. The group color table is a dictionary of overrides to these default color assignments. This allows particular important groups to be assigned distinctive colors. The group color table is saved with the hip file. The current color table can be queried with cite16†hou.hipFile.groupColorTable .\nL192: The supplied `color_table` must be a `dict` of `str` to cite14†hou.Color . The following example will cause groups names 'special_group' to appear with a red overlay:\nL193: \nL194:     color_table = { 'special_group' : hou.Color([1,0,0]) }\nL195:     hou.hipFile.setGroupColorTable(color_table)\nL196: \nL197: \nL198: Sets a dictionary of color overrides for the viewport group list.\nL199: \nL200: `saveMode() ` → cite17†hou.saveMode L201: \nL202: Return the save mode of the current scene, either cite18†hou.saveMode.Binary or cite19†hou.saveMode.Text .\nL203: Return the save mode of the current scene, either hou.saveMode.Binary or hou.saveMode.Text.\nL204: \nL205: `setSaveMode(save_mode) `\nL206: \nL207: Set the save mode of the current scene to either cite18†hou.saveMode.Binary or cite19†hou.saveMode.Text .\nL208: \nL209: Set the save mode of the current scene to either hou.saveMode.Binary or hou.saveMode.Text.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nhou.hda (https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/hom/hou/hda.html)\nciteturn4view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/hom/hou/hda.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 3018\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nhou.Parm (https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/hom/hou/Parm.html)\nciteturn4view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/hom/hou/Parm.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 4133\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0ffaf-55f6-7e90-888e-467b5e9f20e7","create_time":1790996130.481906}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
