{"timestamp":"2026-10-03T02:55:38.380Z","ordinal":273,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a0ffb0-498c-7890-b260-9c50e7f0ab30","call_id":"call_53C9wivoTGXatLD5mgVCBjfq","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 2.0 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"hou.hda (https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/hom/hou/hda.html)\nciteturn5view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/hom/hou/hda.html\",\"pattern\":\"installFile(\"}); Total lines: 3018\nL5: ## Functions cite3†¶ L6: \nL7: `definitionsInFile(file_path) ` → `tuple ` of cite4†hou.HDADefinition L8: \nL9: Return all the digital asset definitions inside an hda file. See cite4†hou.HDADefinition for more information.\nL10: Raises cite5†hou.OperationFailed if `file_path` does not refer to a valid hda file.\nL11: \nL12:     # Print the node types defined by digital assets in $HOME/houdiniX.Y/hda/OPcustom.hda:\nL13:     >>> import os\nL14:     >>> my_hda_file = \"%s/hda/OPcustom.hda\" % hou.homeHoudiniDirectory()\nL15:     >>> for definition in hou.hda.definitionsInFile(my_hda_file):\nL16:     ...     print definition.nodeTypeCategory().name() + \"/\" + definition.nodeTypeName()\nL17:     Sop/gcoggeo\nL18:     Sop/gCogFlatGeo\nL19:     Sop/gDivideAtCentroid\nL20:     Object/gAxle\nL21:     Object/gCog\nL22: Return all the digital asset definitions inside an hda file.\nL23: \nL24: `installFile(file_path, oplibraries_file=None, change_oplibraries_file=True, force_use_assets=False) `\nL25: \nL26: Install all the node types defined in an hda file into the current Houdini session. This function is equivalent to File ▸ Install Digital Asset Library… in Houdini.\nL27: \nL28: file_path\nL29: \nL30: The hda file to load.\nL31: \nL32: oplibraries_file\nL33: The path to an OPlibraries file or `None`. OPlibraries files are text files containing lists of hda files to load on startup. When Houdini starts up it reads all the OPlibraries files it finds in the Houdini path and loads all the hda files listed in them. By creating OPlibraries files in `$HOME/houdiniX.Y`, `$HSITE/houdiniX.Y`, `$JOB`, etc. you can create libraries that are specific to a particular user, studio, job, etc.\nL34: \nL35: This parameter is only meaningful when `change_oplibraries_file` is `True`.\nL36: Note that OPlibraries are only used when the Use OPlibraries files to find HDAS checkbox in the Configuration tab of the Operator Type Manager is checked.\nL37: \nL38: If `None` or “Current HIP File”, the hda file is loaded only into the current Houdini session. The file name will also be saved in the Hip file so that when the same Hip file is loaded, the hda file will also be loaded automatically.\nL39: If “Scanned Asset Library Directories”, this is equivalent to the installation menu entry in the Install Digital Asset Library dialog.\nL40: \nL41: change_oplibraries_file\nL42: \nL43: When `oplibraries_file` is not `None` and this parameter is `True`, Houdini will modify the OPlibraries file, adding the hda file to it.\nL44: \nL45: force_use_assets\nL46: When `True`, ensure that the definitions inside the hda file are current. If they would not otherwise provide the current definition, they are marked as preferred to ensure they are current. See cite6†hou.HDADefinition.isPreferred for more information.\nL47: \nL48: Note that, if you do not store the path to the hda file in an OPlibraries file, Houdini will store it in the current Houdini session. So, when you load a hip file, it will try to load the hda files that it references.\nL49: Install all the node types defined in an hda file into the current Houdini session.\nL50: \nL51: `uninstallFile(file_path, oplibraries_file=None, change_oplibraries_file=True) `\nL52: \nL53: Uninstall an hda file and all the node type definitions it provides from the current Houdini session. The hda file and its contents on disk are unchanged.\nL54: You can set `file_path` to the special name `\"Embedded\"` to refer to the digital assets embedded in the current hip file. The following example removes any embedded digital asset definitions from the current Houdini session:\nL55: \nL56:     hou.hda.uninstallFile(\"Embedded\")\nL57: \nL58: \nL59: If `oplibraries_file` is not `None` and `change_oplibraries_file` is `True`, Houdini will remove the path to the hda from the specified OPlibraries file. See cite7†hou.hda.installFile for more information about these parameters.\nL60: If all the definitions of a node type are uninstalled, any instances of that node type will warn that they are using an incomplete asset definition. They will, however, retain their parameter values as spare parameters. Installing an hda file with the missing node type will restore those node instances and remove the warnings.\nL61: \nL62: See also cite8†hou.HDADefinition.destroy .\nL63: \nL64: Uninstall an hda file and all the node type definitions it provides from the current Houdini session.\nL65: \nL66: `reloadFile(file_path) `\nL67: Reload the contents of an hda file, loading any updated digital asset definitions inside it.\nL68: \nL69: You only need to call this function if an hda file was modified from outside the current Houdini session.\nL70: \nL71: Reload the contents of an hda file, loading any updated digital asset definitions inside it.\nL72: \nL73: `installFiles(file_paths, oplibraries_file=None, change_oplibraries_file=True, force_use_assets=False) `\nL74: Batch install all the node types defined in the list of hda files into the current Houdini session. This function is equivalent to File ▸ Install Digital Asset Library… in Houdini.\nL75: \nL76: file_paths\nL77: \nL78: The list of hda files to install.\nL79: \nL80: See cite7†hou.hda.installFile for more information about installing files.\nL81: \nL82: Batch install all the node types defined in the list of hda files into the current Houdini session.\nL83: \nL84: `uninstallFiles(file_paths, oplibraries_file=None, change_oplibraries_file=True) `\nL85: Batch uninstall a list of hda file and all the node type definitions it provides from the current Houdini session. The hda files and their contents on disk are unchanged.\nL86: \nL87: file_paths\nL88: \nL89: The list of hda files to uninstall.\nL90: \nL91: See cite9†hou.hda.uninstallFile for more information about uninstalling files.\nL92: \nL93: See also cite8†hou.HDADefinition.destroy .\nL94: \nL95: Batch uninstall a list of hda file and all the node type definitions it provides from the current Houdini session.\nL96: \nL97: `reloadFiles(file_paths) `\nL98: Batch reload the contents of a list of hda file, loading any updated digital asset definitions inside them.\nL99: \nL100: You only need to call this function if the hda files were modified from outside the current Houdini session.\nL101: \nL102: file_paths\nL103: \nL104: The list of hda files to reload.\nL105: \nL106: See also cite10†hou.hda.reloadFile .\nL107: \nL108: Batch reload the contents of a list of hda file, loading any updated digital asset definitions inside them.\nL109: \nL110: `reloadAllFiles(rescan=True) `\nL111: Reload the digital asset files and update asset definitions in the current session.\nL112: \nL113: If the `rescan` is true, Houdini will check the hda directories for any new hda files and load them too.\nL114: \nL115: See also cite10†hou.hda.reloadFile .\nL116: \nL117: Reload the digital asset files and update asset definitions in the current session.\nL118: \nL119: `reloadNamespaceOrder() `\nL120: Check `HOUDINI_OPNAMESPACE_HIERARCHY` environment variable and rebuild the node type preference order that determines the node type to use when only unqualified root name is used in scripts, or, when Tab menu settings specify to show only a single preferred entry among several potential choices from different namespaces.\nL121: Check HOUDINI_OPNAMESPACE_HIERARCHY environment variable and rebuild the node type preference order that determines the node type to use when only unqualified root name is used in scripts, or, when Tab menu settings specify to show only a single preferred entry among several potential choices from different namespaces.\nL122: \nL123: `loadedFiles() ` → `tuple ` of `str `\nL124: \nL125: Return a tuple of paths to the hda files that are loaded into the current Houdini session.\nL126: This method is can be approximately implemented as follows:\nL127: \nL128:     def loadedFiles():\nL129:         '''Return a list of hda files loaded into this Houdini session.'''\nL130:         # Look through all the node types, and for those that have digital\nL131:         # asset definitions, remember the hda file containing the definition.\nL132:         result = []\nL133:         for category in hou.nodeTypeCategories().values():\nL134:             for node_type in category.nodeTypes().values():\nL135:                 definition = node_type.definition()\nL136:                 if definition is None:\nL137:                     continue\nL138:                 if definition.libraryFilePath() not in result:\nL139:                     result.append(definition.libraryFilePath())\nL140:         return result\nL141: See cite11†hou.HDADefinition.isCurrent for an example.\nL142: \nL143: Return a tuple of paths to the hda files that are loaded into the current Houdini session.\nL144: \nL145: `expandToDirectory(file_path, directory_path) `\nL146: \nL147: Expand the contents of the hda file in `file_path` into the directory `directory_path`. If the directory does not already exist it is created.\nL148: When expanding an hda file, Houdini puts each digital asset definition in the file into its own directory. As well, it puts each section inside a definition into its own file. Each directory inside the expanded file tree contains a `Sections.List` file that maps the actual file or directory names into the section names, since section names ma